# A terminal too narrow for what the app wants to draw.
#
# Every column here is measured in cells, and every one of them has to give way
# in a fixed order rather than spilling past the edge. A row that overflows
# does not wrap -- it pushes the columns after it right, on that row alone, and
# the board stops lining up.
#
# A name that does not fit ends in a single `…`, which costs a cell of its own.
# The weather still fits at this width and stays on the rule: `too-narrow` is
# the fixture where it does not.
#
# 44 cells is narrower than any real terminal. That is the point: the fixture
# has to reach the case, and a width nobody uses is the cheapest way to.
cache ../cache.db
date 2026-09-11
time 08:00
offset -04:00
size 44 14

# Down to a board, where the most columns compete for the least room.
enter
type 44
enter

# The direction is named. A bare enter takes whatever the slice sorts first.
type hurdman
enter
enter

# And a stop search, where the name, the pole number, the destination and the
# route list all want the same cells.
esc
type billings
